WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.334
-Welcome, Mo!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:04.434
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:06.901
-Welcome back.
-Wow.

00:00:06.901 --> 00:00:08.601
I am just so excited.

00:00:08.601 --> 00:00:10.534
-It is great to have you
in person.

00:00:10.534 --> 00:00:13.400
-To be in this time slot!
-Yes!

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.334
-It's just so great
for my demographic.

00:00:15.334 --> 00:00:16.834
It really is.
-It really -- Your demographic.

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:18.567
-It's perfect.
-It's perfect to be -- Yeah.

00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:19.901
After midnight, yeah.

00:00:19.901 --> 00:00:21.767
-No, because this is when they
want a glass of water.

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:23.400
-Oh, right at this moment,
right now.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.601
They're ready, they're ready.

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:28.534
As you know, I'm
a giant fan of your work.

00:00:28.534 --> 00:00:31.133
Everything --
"Pigeon Drive the Bus,"

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:33.067
Elephant and Piggie are my jam.

00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:34.968
-Oh, that's sweet, thank you.

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.734
-"City Dog, Country Frog."
-Yeah.

00:00:36.734 --> 00:00:39.100
-That's my jam too.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.934
I have your work on my socks.
-Oh!

00:00:40.934 --> 00:00:42.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:45.033
I love it, very nice.

00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:46.400
I just gotta tell you
a quick story

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.667
because I'm a fan of yours,

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.834
and I was so excited when I did
the show the first time.

00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:52.200
-Thank you.
-The next day I'm walking

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.167
my dog, and Peg and Fred are on
their porch and they say,

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:58.968
"Hey, Mo! You were great
on 'Fallon' last night."

00:00:58.968 --> 00:01:00.267
I said, "Great, that's
really nice.

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:01.434
Jimmy is a good guy."

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:03.234
And he goes, "he's a great man!"

00:01:03.234 --> 00:01:05.701
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.701 --> 00:01:07.968
-What? Wow, he corrected you.

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:10.601
-Very angry fan.
-Peg and Fred?

00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:12.567
-Peg and Fred. They love you.
-Peg and Fred, I love you.

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:14.601
Thank you for correcting him,
yeah.

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.100
I'm not a good guy.
-You're a great man!

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.434
-I'm a great man!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:23.200
I love Peg and Fred.
Thanks, guys.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.934
You know, I heard that you
started your own comic strip

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:28.634
when you were seven years old?

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:30.901
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, I guess.

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:33.901
You know, when I was five,
I wrote Charles Schulz a letter,

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:35.300
and I said, "Dear Mr. Schulz,

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.834
can I have your job
when you're dead?"

00:01:36.834 --> 00:01:39.200
[ Laughter ]
-So sweet of you, so sweet.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.267
He must've loved that, yeah.

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:43.100
-Well, he not died
for a long time.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.334
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:45.834
And I was a little --
-"He not died."

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:48.534
-I was a little frustrated.
But then in second grade,

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:50.033
I was like, "Well, I gotta do
something about it."

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:52.834
-Yeah.
-And the second grade bully --

00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:54.300
-You were gonna kill
Charles Schulz.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.367
-Yeah, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:58.133
I was a little too small.
-Yeah, yeah, no.

00:01:58.133 --> 00:01:59.801
He's a legend, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:59.801 --> 00:02:03.534
No, but second grade bully said,
you know, "I want a gag a day."

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:07.334
And so I wrote a comic strip for
for this guy.

00:02:07.334 --> 00:02:10.434
And if he liked it, then he'd
kinda let me off the hook.

00:02:10.434 --> 00:02:12.267
And if he didn't...

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:14.334
It's like working in television,
Jimmy, is what it was like.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.133
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly,
yeah.

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:19.501
But you started just making
these strips, and you go,

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:21.567
"Oh, this is something."

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:25.234
-You know, I've always wanted to
draw and be funny.

00:02:25.234 --> 00:02:28.300
I mean, that, for me, seemed
like the most magical thing,

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.534
and "Peanuts" was great for me
because, you know, Charlie Brown

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:35.000
had it worse than I did, so...
-Yeah, he did.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.300
-Yeah.
-This special now,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.133
people get to see this --
"Naked Mole Rat."

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:40.067
-Yes.
-Basically the idea of this is

00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:41.667
be true to yourself
no matter what.

00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:43.634
-Yeah, so "The Naked Mole Rat
Gets Dressed,"

00:02:43.634 --> 00:02:45.300
it originally was a book.
-Yeah.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.167
-And that was about 15 years
ago, and what happened actually

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:52.100
is Charles Schulz did pass, and
I became friends with his widow.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.934
And she gave me one of his nibs,
which was the little piece

00:02:55.934 --> 00:02:59.567
of metal you put in the pen that
you dip in the ink and you draw.

00:02:59.567 --> 00:03:01.334
So this is the little piece
of metal

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:03.567
that he did "Peanuts" with.
-No.

00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:06.334
[ Audience ohs ]
-Obviously, yes, right, amazing.

00:03:06.334 --> 00:03:09.133
And so I had this nib,
and I was like,

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:10.667
"I have to do something special
with this.

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:14.167
I really have to dig deep
and ask myself some questions."

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:17.434
And out of it came this book
"Naked Mole Rat Gets Dressed."

00:03:17.434 --> 00:03:19.801
-Yeah.
-So 15 years ago -- And then we

00:03:19.801 --> 00:03:22.100
made it a theater rock opera,
and now we've made it

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.634
an animated rock opera special.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:26.968
-Yeah, and isn't fantastic,
though? I love that.

00:03:26.968 --> 00:03:29.467
So it's a naked mole rat, and he
just -- Everyone's naked.

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:32.734
-Everyone is 100% naked
500% of the time.

00:03:32.734 --> 00:03:34.100
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.367
That's what mole rats do, yeah.
-And Wilbur decides to do

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:39.834
something radical
and try on clothes,

00:03:39.834 --> 00:03:42.567
and the culture goes nuts!

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:46.067
and so this is, for me, it's --
it's not so much even

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:48.701
a coming-out story,
it's an accepting-in story.

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:51.701
-Yeah.
-I think it's really the job of

00:03:51.701 --> 00:03:55.334
the us's to ask to the them's
like, "Why not?

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.501
How special, how magic that you

00:03:57.501 --> 00:03:59.334
are different
in these incredible ways?

00:03:59.334 --> 00:04:01.367
And I want to learn from that
and be part of that."

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:05.300
So it's all about this naked
culture trying to figure out

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.968
what to do with clothes.

00:04:06.968 --> 00:04:10.200
-You know, I don't want to make
your head big or anything,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.701
but a lot of people are
comparing you to Mr. Rogers.

00:04:13.701 --> 00:04:15.367
Have you heard that at all?

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:18.100
-No, that's very flattering.
-Yeah.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.501
-They are wrong.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.501 --> 00:04:21.300
-Yeah.
-So you just tell them,

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.734
if you run across them,
just say no.

00:04:23.734 --> 00:04:25.834
-No, he's not.
-But --

00:04:25.834 --> 00:04:27.200
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.934
What I love about Fred Rogers
and what I aspire to do

00:04:31.934 --> 00:04:36.567
is to always have curiosity
and to remember the idea

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:39.300
that growing up
and discovering new stuff

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.367
and becoming more you
and becoming a better friend

00:04:42.367 --> 00:04:44.100
is not a childhood activity.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.267
We are so lucky.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:47.567
It is a lifelong activity.

00:04:47.567 --> 00:04:50.367
We get to grow and learn

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:53.501
and be more true
to ourselves every day.

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:54.667
-Yeah, that is true.

00:04:54.667 --> 00:04:56.033
-It's a miracle.
-Yeah, that's amazing.

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:58.234
That is correct.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:00.901
This is on HBO Max.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:05:00.901 --> 00:05:04.300
Here's a peek at Mo Willems'
"Naked Mole Rat Gets Dressed:

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.200
The Underground Rock
Experience."

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.634
Take a look.

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:09.501
♪♪

00:05:09.501 --> 00:05:13.033
-♪ What is that? ♪

00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:16.367
♪ Is that a hat? ♪

00:05:16.367 --> 00:05:20.734
♪ Would it be wrong
to try it on?

00:05:20.734 --> 00:05:23.100
Oh, wow!

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.300
♪ It feels good,
like a brand-new friend ♪

00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:34.534
♪ If I had a friend that was
a cotton-poly blend ♪

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:36.100
Ohh!

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:40.534
♪ I feel more like myself
with something on ♪

00:05:40.534 --> 00:05:44.467
♪ But what if's something
that's frowned upon? ♪

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:47.167
Eep!

00:05:47.167 --> 00:05:49.100
-I can't wait.
Mo Willems, everybody.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.601
Mo Willems'
"Naked Mole Rat Gets Dressed:

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:53.467
The Underground Rock Experience"

